  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Tuesday...\n\nThe next 7 days is a tale of two weather regimes.\n\nToday through Friday will remain quite warm & dry as flat ridging\nwith above normal heights dominates the northern Rockies and high\nplains. There is a weak cold front dropping out of Canada this\nmorning that will shift winds to the NE-E and provide a bit of\nbackdoor cooling, along with higher humidities. Thursday is \nlooking a little warmer & drier as east winds slacken. Friday is \nof greater concern with regard to fire weather as SW flow \namplifies and downslope winds/warming become enhanced ahead of an \napproaching Canadian front. Western foothills should see gusts to \n40 mph along with RHs as low as the single digits to lower teens, \nand temps back to the 80s. One beneficial item of note is that the\nfropa itself won't arrive until early Saturday, so shifting/ \nfrontal winds will not be an issue in our area on Friday. There \nmay, however, be modest mountain wave potential in the lee of the \nBeartooths & Bighorns early Saturday. Model soundings show only a \nvague signal for mountain waves but this is something to keep an \neye on. \n\nCold front Saturday morning will bring a shift to brisk N-NE winds\nand cooling thru the day. The cooling will be noticeable. At the \nsame time, a potent upper low will drop from BC to the PacNW. \nShower chances will increase over the west during the day, but the\nmain show looks to begin Saturday night as large scale ascent and\ndeepening upslope flow combine with falling temps to bring a risk\nof foothills and lower elevation snow for the first time this \nfall.\n\nThere remains plenty of uncertainty with this system, which is \nexpected to impact us in some fashion from Saturday night through \nMonday, and perhaps a bit longer. High confidence exists for much \ncooler temps (highs in 30s & 40s Sunday & Monday) and a healthy \ndose of precipitation (which should feature mountains and western \nfoothills snowfall). Mid level E-SE winds favor western foothills \nfor greatest precipitation potential. High uncertainty exists with\nexact track of the low and magnitude of precipitation we will \nsee, in addition to surface temps and how much snow will be able \nto accumulate after a stretch of very warm weather. One thing \nnoted in today's models was a lingering weak split trof over \nwestern MT through Monday, which is important as it suggests the \nlow will not yet be completely cut off to our south, keeps ascent \ngoing and cooler air in place. For these reasons confidence in \naccumulating snowfall is a little higher now than it was \nyesterday.\n\nSpeaking of confidence here are some current probabilities. The \nchance of 1+ inches of snow is 10% over the east, 40% at Billings \nand 70-80% along western foothills. The chance of 6+ inches of \nsnow is non-zero at Billings (10%) and 35-45% along the western \nfoothills. Western mountains will of course see more snow than \nthis. Per ensembles, precip is expected to be near an inch in our \nwest tapering to a half inch central parts and near 0.15\" in the \neast. Of course, this is subject to change. If ascent and upslope \nwinds remain favorable through much of Monday, and if moisture \nfrom remnants of Hurricane Rachel become involved, we may be \nlooking at significant accumulating wet snowfall across western \nfoothills. Potential impacts include travel and weighted down \nleafy trees. Also, it is likely that most of the region sees its \nfirst freeze of the fall. Please monitor the forecast if you are \nplanning to travel or engage in outdoor activities Saturday night \nthrough Monday.\n\nLight precip may linger through Monday night into early Tuesday,\nbut overall Tuesday should begin the trend back to warmer and\ndrier. Look for highs in the mid 40s to mid 50s.\n\nJKL",
